# What a reconcilable tree owes the reconciler, and the whole of what tea_core
# knows about trees. The types named here are `W`, `List[W]`, `Int` and `Rel`;
# there is no widget, no style, no tag and no message anywhere in this package.
#
# Two language facts shape the contract, and both are diagnostics this file was
# written around rather than preferences:
#
#   - `type Op[W: Tree]` is rejected -- "type declarations cannot constrain
#     their type parameters" -- and an unbounded projection `W.Attr` is
#     rejected too. So an op may not carry a vocabulary-defined attribute
#     payload, and every payload in `Op` is built from types core already
#     knows. That is why the in-place update carries a whole node
#     (`SetSelf(w)`) rather than a style.
#   - The orphan rule is per module, so `impl Tree[Widget[M]]` cannot live
#     here. It lives in the module declaring the vocabulary; for the terminal
#     that is `tea_term/widget`.
#
# A vocabulary must also have structural `==`, which the reconciler demands as
# an ordinary `Eq` bound rather than as a member of this trait. Structural
# equality is free for any non-function type, so the bound is really the rule
# "a node holds no callbacks" -- and leaving it a bound rather than an
# obligation means a vocabulary that breaks it is rejected where it reaches the
# reconciler instead of quietly implementing a member that lies.

## How a vocabulary says two nodes at the same address are related. Closed, and
## about semantics rather than hosts: the reconciler asks only "may I diff these
## in place, and did the node's own data change".
pub type Rel =
  | Unrelated
  | Same
  | SelfDiffers
derive Show

## The contract. Three members, one per thing the reconciler does: walk
## children, decide a pair's fate, and rebuild a node around new children.
pub trait Tree[W] {
  ## This node's children in order; a leaf has none. `diff` pairs these by
  ## index and `apply` addresses through them, so an index in a `Patch.path`
  ## is an index into exactly this list.
  fn kids(w: W) -> List[W]

  ## `w` with its children replaced. Must be total: a leaf handed children is
  ## expected to ignore them and return itself, which is what keeps `apply`
  ## total against an address that does not exist.
  fn rekid(w: W, ks: List[W]) -> W

  ## Whether the pair may be diffed in place, and whether the node's own data
  ## (everything that is not a child) changed. Only ever called on a pair the
  ## reconciler has already found unequal, so a vocabulary never has to
  ## re-compare fields the `==` shortcut already settled.
  fn relate(old: W, new: W) -> Rel
}
